Durarara!! AU
Chapter 46 – Part 1
First class hummed like money that had learned to be quiet.
Leona had her laptop open against the seat-back, earbuds in one ear, the other listening to the cabin. Adolf had a beer he was not supposed to need at that altitude. Maria had her boots on the extra space like she owned the cloud. Carlos watched the aisle the way he watched doors.
Leona turned the screen.
“Guys. Check this.”
The video was last night. Ikebukuro grain. A white-and-black rider on a building wall. A lab coat. Muzzle flash. A body folding.
“That’s Curtis! Homes!”
Maria leaned in so hard her seat belt complained.
“I don’t recognize the mask he’s wearing,” Leona said, already sure anyway. “But I suspect he got a new one in Japan.”
The shot hit.
Maria’s voice went sharp.
“Oh fuck! They just shot him in the head! Why’s he protecting a lab coat?”
The clip kept going. Black smoke. A scythe. A helmeted figure that did not move like a person in a costume hauled the rider off the concrete.
Adolf’s beer stopped halfway.
“Who’s the headless woman with the scythe that just grabbed him?”
Carlos finally looked at the screen.
“Play it again.”
Leona did.
Same fall. Same lab coat. Same impossible catch.
“That’s him,” Leona said, quieter. “Walk’s the same. Shoulders are the same. He left and didn’t say goodbye and now he’s on the internet eating a bullet for a scientist.”
Maria sat back, color high.
“If that’s Curtis, the headshot should’ve dropped him for real. He got up in the comments. People think it’s fake.”
“It’s not fake.” Adolf’s mouth was a line. “That’s the same idiot who used to bounce knives off a counter and call it dinner. He found another forest. This one has cameras.”
Carlos tapped the pause on the scythe.
“Headless. That’s not a filter. We land, we find him before the city files him under dead.”
Leona shut the laptop halfway, like the cabin might lean in.
“Japan,” she said. “He took Justice’s half of the plan and ran. Now he’s on a wall with a new face and a woman who doesn’t have one.”
The plane kept flying toward a city that had already filmed their missing brother.
Four seats in first class stopped being a vacation.
The Undead were coming.
Carlos did not look away from the paused scythe.
“You think that woman is like one of them dullahans him and Justice told stories about.”
The first-class cabin kept serving warmth and pretzels. None of it reached the row.
Leona snorted, then didn’t finish the joke.
“They used to talk like that when they thought we couldn’t hear. Heads under arms. Coaches. The nice ones and the ones that peel you.” She flicked the screen. “This one has a motorcycle and a lab coat problem. That’s new.”
Maria hugged her elbows.
“If it is, that’s why he didn’t stay dead on the clip. Forest rules. Not YouTube rules.”
Adolf set the beer down untouched.
“They also said dullahans carry the head. This one doesn’t. That’s the part that would have made Justice sit up.” A thin breath. “Curtis would still walk toward it. He always walked toward the story.”
Carlos nodded once.
“Then we don’t land and ask the city. We land and ask him. If he made a friend out of one, I want to see the friend before the friend decides we’re prey.”
Leona opened the laptop again, just enough to freeze the helmet.
“Ikebukuro,” she muttered. “He picked a nest with cameras and fae. Typical.”
The plane crossed another stretch of ocean.
Four people who had never been allowed in the deep forest sat in leather seats and treated a viral video like a field report from two boys who used to come home smelling like leaves and secrets.
A dullahan on a Japanese street.
A brother in a mask they didn’t recognize.
The stories had grown legs and bought a plane ticket.
Adolf watched the frozen helmet like he had seen the shape of it before.
“In the gulag. Justice and Curtis were shot at. He has that helmet on. Looks good. Expensive too. The helmet did its job.” He turned the cup in his fingers. “I remember when we escaped. Justice and him were laughing. Gulag broke some of the toughest men I knew.”
Carlos’s jaw ticked.
“It wasn’t the same when the cartel took him and my papa. They were more like kids when they came back. Something was stripped.”
Maria looked at him, then at the screen.
“When they came back from Russia with Adolf, it wasn’t the same as when they came back from Mexico with you, babe.”
Leona didn’t soften it.
“No. Because they didn’t have powers yet. Whatever marked them, the powers didn’t come out right away.” Her voice dropped under the engine noise. “Curtis used to sob at night because his powers didn’t activate in time to save your father.”
Carlos stared at the seat-back.
“I know. Homes wouldn’t let it go for a long time.”
Nobody reached for a joke.
The video sat there with a bullet and a lab coat and a woman with no head, and under it was an older clip none of them needed to play: two boys coming out of a place that had taken a father and given back children. Russia later, laughter like a cracked bell, Adolf walking out with them. Mexico first, quieter, Carlos counting who didn’t get on the truck.
“Helmet would’ve been nice then,” Maria muttered.
“Helmet wouldn’t have been enough then,” Adolf said. “They didn’t have the other thing yet. The thing that mops blood and makes roses. That came after. Late. Useless late, if you ask him on a bad night.”
Leona shut the laptop.
“He’s in Japan eating a headshot for a stranger in a coat. That’s the same boy who cried in a motel because the lock opened too slow.” She looked at Carlos. “We find him. We don’t start with papa. We start with you left. Then we get to the mask.”
Carlos nodded once.
“He still owes the night he wouldn’t sleep. I don’t need him to pay it. I need him in a room where I can see he’s not a clip.”
The plane kept eating ocean.
Four seats held a gulag laugh, a cartel silence, and a brother who had learned his powers too late for one man and just in time for a lab coat on a Japanese wall.
The Undead did not toast.
They waited for the landing light.
Carlos looked at Maria.
Maria didn’t even let him finish the look.
“No, babe. I haven’t practiced my Japanese. It’s difficult.”
“But isn’t the dialect the same as—”
Maria’s fist tagged the top of his head. Not cruel. Educational.
Adolf and Leona laughed like the cabin had finally given them something that wasn’t a headshot.
Adolf lifted his cup.
“Comrade. You don’t understand the difference between the people out there. It’s the same as when you thought Curtis was Mexican cause his skin tone.”
Carlos rubbed his skull, wounded in his pride and nowhere else.
“Yeah, but he knew Spanish like he spoke it all his life.”
Leona leaned on the armrest.
“You’re not wrong for thinking he’s Mexican if all you had was a look. Mexico’s full of Indigenous people. Different nations. Different languages. Spaniards showed up and wrote their name on top.” She tipped her chin. “Curtis is from the Northwest Territories. First Nations. Different land. Different people. Europeans wrote their name on top of his, too. That’s the rhyme. Not the same book.”
Carlos squinted.
“So Japanese isn’t Spanish with extra steps.”
Maria hit him again, lighter.
“That’s what I said.”
Adolf was still grinning.
“He learned Spanish because he lives in languages. Not because your airport guessed his face. He learned Japanese the same way. You land talking like a cartoon and he will clock you in three words.”
Leona opened the laptop a crack, just the paused helmet.
“We find him first. We do not ask a taxi driver to translate hey homes you got shot. Maria studies on the descent. Carlos does not invent a dialect.”
Carlos muttered into his hand.
“I was being efficient.”
“You were being wrong,” Maria said, and kissed the spot she had punched.
The plane hummed.
Four people who had survived a gulag laugh and a cartel silence argued about language like it was the only safe thing left, while a First Nations boy they still called homes slept a city away with a month-old hole and a mask they had only just seen.
Ikebukuro did not know they were coming.
Curtis did not know the first-class row had already decided he was alive.
Leona shut the laptop for good this time.
“Good thing no one told that snake of a bitch Katlyn. None of you tell him. She’s looking for him either. He doesn’t need someone who manipulated him.”
Adolf’s humor left his mouth.
“Agreed.”
Maria nodded hard enough her earrings moved.
“If she gets a pin on this flight I will throw the pin in the ocean. He already cried over her once. We’re not delivering the sequel with the mask footage.”
Carlos rubbed the sore spot on his head and meant a different promise.
“He doesn’t hear her name from us. If she already sniffed Japan, that’s her problem. We don’t confirm. We don’t ‘accidentally’ mention a video.”
Leona looked at each of them until it stuck.
“He’s a month out from Justice. He’s on a wall taking a bullet. Last thing he needs is the girl who taught him love was a leash walking into Ikebukuro smiling.”
They all agreed.
First class went quiet again, the easy kind this time, the kind that had a rule in it.
Katlyn stayed a closed door over the Pacific.
The Undead kept their brother’s new city off a snake’s map, and the plane kept carrying four people who could be loud, wrong about dialects, and still know which name was not allowed to land with them.
Carlos started laughing first, the kind that got him in trouble in quieter rooms.
“Hey. Didn’t Justice mention when Curtis was younger he took a pass at some ancient woman in the forest.”
The row went with it.
Maria covered her mouth and failed. Leona’s forehead hit the seat in front of her. Adolf actually used the beer.
“So a dullahan in Japan fits him perfectly,” Adolf said, wiping his eye. “Of course he does. Boy spends his childhood flirting with things that should eat him, then he moves to a city and finds one with a motorcycle.”
Leona wheezed.
“Justice swore Curtis tried to be polite about it. Ma’am. Your head. Like that was a greeting.”
“And still walked closer,” Maria added. “That’s the part. He doesn’t run from the story. He asks if it wants coffee.”
Carlos shook his head, grinning at the closed laptop.
“Headless woman pulls him off a wall after a headshot and we’re surprised. That’s not a new hobby. That’s the old one with better cameras.”
Adolf lifted the cup toward the hull.
“To homes. Consistent. Terrible. Alive.”
They laughed until the seatbelt light felt like a punchline.
Somewhere under them, Ikebukuro had a dullahan, a mask, and a boy who had always had a type that did not show up in yearbooks.
The Undead toasted a rumor Justice had told over a fire, and for a minute the ocean was allowed to be funny.